% holding-decision-note: Held as a link only (README, "Cultures that protect their music"): the guide's terms cover learning and performing, not copying the score here.
% holding-blocked: Still needed: the composer's written permission before any notation, words or audio are posted on the site (the guide allows learning, performing and sharing for education, not reposting); and a review by the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre or by the composer of the credit, the story and how the class sings it. Neither has happened. Until then the class learns it from the CBC and MusiCounts pages.
% holding-kind: link
% holding-kind-note: No notes or words by design: the composer, CBC Music and MusiCounts share the song for teaching on their own pages, under terms that allow learning, performing and sharing for education but not reposting. Link to their pages.
% source-url: https://www.cbc.ca/music/events/canadian-music-class-challenge/learn-how-to-sing-ode-min-giizis-strawberry-moon-1.6158619 CBC Music: learn how to sing Ode'min Giizis (not read here; CBC refuses automated readers)
% source-url: https://musicounts.s3.amazonaws.com/documents/MusiCounts_Singing_In_Indigenous_Languages_final.pdf MusiCounts and CBC Music, Singing in Indigenous Languages (2024), pp. 4-6: the song and the terms
% performance-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=USol6VcHmb0 Tara Williamson, Ode'min Giizis (Strawberry Moon) (CBC Music Class)
% dossier-function: The 2021 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song. The guide says the title means "Strawberry Moon" and the song "pays homage to the ways in which Annishinaabe people track the seasons through the moon. The strawberry moon marks the beginning of June, and is a reminder of friendship, sweetness, and love." Williamson is a singer-songwriter from the Opaskwayak Cree Nation in Manitoba.
% dossier-elements: Not transcribed here; read it from the material on the CBC page.
% dossier-practice: Learn the Anishinaabemowin from the teaching video. A season song: it fits a lesson on how the moons mark the year.
% dossier-accompaniment: The guide (p. 6) asks that traditional Indigenous instruments be left to Indigenous people: "Our instruments are considered sacred" and, if an educator wishes to include them, "it is highly recommended to reserve that role for an Indigenous person, whether that be a student that is knowledgeable on the matter or bringing in a Knowledge Keeper from your community." The Band plays nothing for this record; no hand drum, rattle or powwow drum from us.
% dossier-lyrics: The words are the songwriter's, in Anishinaabemowin; not copied here.
% dossier-honesty: Composed and owned: a song by a living songwriter, not traditional. Nothing here is ours.
% dossier-licence: Te Credit wording to use: "Ode'min Giizis (Strawberry Moon)" by Tara Williamson; the 2021 CBC Canadian Music Class Challenge Indigenous-language song (CBC Music and MusiCounts) (to confirm with the songwriters).
% dossier-respect: A contemporary song shared for classes. Teach only what the song, its teaching video and the guide say about it; deeper teachings about the moons belong to Anishinaabe teachers. Credit the songwriter.
% dossier-review: Tara Williamson for the credit and any posting; an Anishinaabemowin speaker for pronunciation; the TDSB Urban Indigenous Education Centre for classroom use. None has reviewed it.
% lesson: Before the lesson: open the CBC page for Ode'min Giizis and its teaching video.
% lesson: Tell the story first: Tara Williamson, a singer-songwriter from the Opaskwayak Cree Nation in Manitoba, wrote this song in Anishinaabemowin. Ode'min Giizis means Strawberry Moon; the guide says the song honours the way Anishinaabe people track the seasons by the moon, and that the strawberry moon marks the beginning of June and is a reminder of friendship, sweetness and love.
% lesson: Pronunciation: learn the Anishinaabemowin from the teaching video, phrase by phrase.
% lesson: Sing from the material on the CBC page, then the whole song. It fits a June lesson or a unit on seasons, using only what the song and its video say about the moons.
% lesson: Accompaniment: none from Indigenous instruments (the guide, p. 6, asks that hand drums, rattles and the big drum be left to an Indigenous student or Knowledge Keeper). If the class wants accompaniment, use only what the printed score gives; the Band stays silent.
% lesson: Close: name the songwriters and the year again, and ask what the class noticed about the language. Do not post or share a recording of the class beyond the school; the terms are "educational purposes only".
